 SF("SHF-105","CrossBuy/BL/TaskReadAuthorizer.cs","UNCLAIMED destination path - narrow handoff to TAB-1 for the B5 kernel-boundary structural move","ALLOWED: receiving TaskReadAuthorization, ITaskReadAuthorizer and TaskReadAuthorizer at this exact path as a STRUCTURAL MOVE out of the Platform kernel, with the namespace corrected from CrossBuy.BL.Platform to CrossBuy.BL, and nothing else. THE MOVE THIS SERVES, stated once so every concern below means the same thing: CrossBuy/BL/Platform/TaskReadAuthorizer.cs and CrossBuy/BL/Platform/EmployeeDocumentOwnerResolver.cs were module-aware implementations sitting inside the Platform kernel. TaskReadAuthorizer named TasksActions and took ITasksAccessService, ITaskSourceVisibility and ITaskSourceReadEntrance; EmployeeDocumentOwnerResolver named HrActions five times. PlatformPermissionVocabularyTests forbids exactly that, because a kernel that names module vocabulary cannot be committed or built without dragging the module access services in with it. Both files therefore MOVED OUT of the kernel to CrossBuy/BL/, namespace CrossBuy.BL - the namespace where TasksActions and HrActions are themselves declared, and where TasksAccessService, TaskService, TaskReportService, TimesheetService and HrAccessService already live. NOTHING ABOUT BEHAVIOUR CHANGED: same types, same members, same signatures, same DI lifetimes, same fail-closed answers. THE INTERFACE MOVES WITH THE IMPLEMENTATION AND THAT IS DELIBERATE: ITaskReadAuthorizer.ApplyAsync takes and returns IQueryable of TaskItem, so it is a Tasks contract and never was a module-neutral kernel one. Leaving it behind would have kept a Tasks entity type in the kernel while pretending the boundary was repaired. BEHAVIOURAL OWNERSHIP DOES NOT MOVE WITH THE FILE: Tasks read authority, the scope layers, source visibility, the source-explicit entrance and every rule about what a task IS remain TAB-5 module concerns. THIS ROW AUTHORISES THE MOVE, NOT THE MODULE: a later change to how task reads are authorized needs TAB-5, not this entry. NOT ALLOWED under this concern: any behavioural change of any kind; any change to a method signature, an interface member, an authorization rule, a permission key, a scope, a company or tenant predicate, a DI lifetime or a registration order; any compatibility shim or forwarding type left behind in BL/Platform; any weakening, renaming, skipping or exclusion in PlatformPermissionVocabularyTests or any other guard; any unrelated using-cleanup, reformatting or refactor; and any SQL, database or UI change. Changes require owner approval (-AllowShared).","The destination path was claimed by NO tab, so -Strict refused it and the move could not land without this entry. TAB-1 because the concern is a platform/kernel boundary repair adjudicated under B5, not a Tasks feature. THE DESTINATION WAS CHOSEN ON MEASUREMENT, not convenience: CrossBuy/BL/TasksCalendar is CLAIMED by TAB-5 and returns DENIED to TAB-1, and granting TAB-1 a path inside another tab-s module directory would be an ownership transfer rather than a narrow handoff. CrossBuy/BL is unclaimed, is where this file-s own dependency ITasksAccessService already lives, and is the namespace TasksActions is declared in - so the implementation joins the module layer it belongs to instead of being parked somewhere neutral. FIRST-MATCH VERIFIED BEFORE WRITING: all 104 existing shared rows were evaluated against this exact path under the checker-s own ConvertTo-PathRegex prefix semantics and none matches it; rows sort by fileId as a string, so this row precedes every later-numbered rule. First and only match."),
